    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the probability that two randomly chosen members from a club of 10 members (5 boys and 5 girls) are both girls, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine the total number of ways to choose 2 members out of 10.**\n\n   We use the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{k} \\), which represents the number of ways to choose \\( k \\) items from \\( n \\) items without regard to order.\n\n   Here, \\( n = 10 \\) and \\( k = 2 \\).\n\n   \\[\n   \\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10!}{2!(10-2)!} = \\frac{10!}{2! \\cdot 8!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2 \\times 1} = 45\n   \\]\n\n   So, there are 45 ways to choose 2 members from 10.\n\n2. **Determine the number of ways to choose 2 girls out of the 5 girls.**\n\n   We again use the combination formula, but this time with \\( n = 5 \\) and \\( k = 2 \\).\n\n   \\[\n   \\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\frac{5!}{2! \\cdot 3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10\n   \\]\n\n   So, there are 10 ways to choose 2 girls from 5.\n\n3. **Calculate the probability that both chosen members are girls.**\n\n   The probability is the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes (choosing 2 girls) to the total number of outcomes (choosing any 2 members).\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Probability} = \\frac{\\text{Number of ways to choose 2 girls}}{\\text{Total number of ways to choose 2 members}} = \\frac{10}{45}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Simplify the fraction.**\n\n   Both the numerator and the denominator can be divided by their greatest common divisor, which is 5.\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10 \\div 5}{45 \\div 5} = \\frac{2}{9}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that two randomly chosen members of the club are both girls is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{9}}\n\\]",
